To protect the application against misuse, we implemented rate limiting on the /api/chat endpoint using flask-limiter, capping requests at 20 per minute per IP address — preventing both accidental abuse and deliberate flooding of our Groq API quota. CORS was locked down from an open wildcard to explicit localhost origins only, ensuring the backend refuses requests from any unintended source. During a cleanup pass, we identified and removed print statements that were inadvertently writing the raw RapidAPI key and full API response bodies to server logs — a real exposure risk if logs were ever stored or forwarded. We also fixed a data mutation bug in the flight service where shared mock data was being modified in place across requests, which could have caused unpredictable results under concurrent load. The application currently does not implement user authentication, as it is a stateless prototype — there are no user accounts or sessions to protect, and all data is scoped to a single browser session with no persistence.